1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,390
Just after a quarter to one in the
morning on March the twenty-first,

2
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:10,310
2005, the US House of Representatives
passed a law about one woman.

3
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:13,070
Within the hour, the
President signed it.

4
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:18,000
It gave a federal court the power to
hear her parents' case again,

5
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:22,470
from the start. And it said it set no
precedent for anyone else.

6
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:26,680
Her name was Terri Schiavo.

7
00:00:27,610 --> 00:00:31,210
Fifteen years earlier, when she was
twenty-six, her heart had stopped

8
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:32,340
at home in Florida.

9
00:00:33,270 --> 00:00:37,520
Paramedics restarted it, but her brain
had gone too long without oxygen.

10
00:00:38,110 --> 00:00:39,180
She never recovered.

11
00:00:41,570 --> 00:00:43,320
She had left nothing in writing.

12
00:00:44,070 --> 00:00:47,390
Her husband, Michael, said she had told
him she wouldn't want to live

13
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:52,720
like that. Her parents, Bob and Mary
Schindler, didn't believe it,

14
00:00:52,970 --> 00:00:54,920
and believed she was still aware.

15
00:00:55,450 --> 00:00:59,030
A judge had sided with her husband,
and her feeding tube had been

16
00:00:59,050 --> 00:01:00,600
removed three days before.

17
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:04,970
The federal courts
declined to step in.

18
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:09,320
She died on March the thirty-first,
thirteen days after the tube was

19
00:01:09,340 --> 00:01:11,230
removed. She was forty-one.

20
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:16,560
Most of us have never written
down what we'd want.

21
00:01:17,090 --> 00:01:21,370
In one large review, only about one
American adult in three had an

22
00:01:21,390 --> 00:01:22,440
advance directive.

23
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:29,060
So, when someone can't speak for
themselves, who should decide when

24
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:30,570
to stop keeping them alive?

25
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:32,870
Their husband or wife?

26
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:35,710
Their parents? A judge?

27
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:40,290
Politicians? And what should
count as their own words?

28
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:47,070
We went through the trial judge's
ruling, the appeals, the guardian's

29
00:01:47,090 --> 00:01:51,940
report, the autopsy, the laws Florida
and Congress passed for her,

30
00:01:52,150 --> 00:01:54,160
and how other countries decide.

31
00:01:54,870 --> 00:01:59,400
At the end, we'll give you a verdict,
and tell you how sure we are.

32
00:02:12,294 --> 00:02:14,514
Terri Schiavo grew up
near Philadelphia.

33
00:02:14,534 --> 00:02:18,094
She married Michael Schiavo at twenty
and moved with him to Florida,

34
00:02:18,114 --> 00:02:21,094
where her parents and her
brother and sister followed.

35
00:02:21,114 --> 00:02:23,514
She worked for an insurance company.

36
00:02:23,534 --> 00:02:27,274
Her family calls her Terri
Schindler Schiavo.

37
00:02:27,294 --> 00:02:31,194
Early on February the twenty-fifth,
1990, her husband heard a thump

38
00:02:31,214 --> 00:02:33,324
and found her on the floor
of their hallway.

39
00:02:33,894 --> 00:02:35,124
Her heart had stopped.

40
00:02:36,824 --> 00:02:39,474
She came out of the coma, but
not into consciousness.

41
00:02:40,184 --> 00:02:43,474
Doctors diagnosed a
persistent vegetative state.

42
00:02:44,364 --> 00:02:48,074
As a Florida appeals court later put
it, that is not simply a coma:

43
00:02:48,584 --> 00:02:53,144
she had cycles of waking and
sleeping, but, the doctors found, no

44
00:02:53,164 --> 00:02:55,994
awareness. She breathed on her own.

45
00:02:56,704 --> 00:02:58,094
She was fed through a tube.

46
00:03:00,594 --> 00:03:03,284
What stopped her heart has
never been established.

47
00:03:04,214 --> 00:03:08,064
Fifteen years later, the medical examiner
said it couldn't be determined.

48
00:03:09,954 --> 00:03:14,134
For three years, her husband and her
parents cared for her together.

49
00:03:14,824 --> 00:03:18,064
A guardian appointed by the court
later described them as virtual

50
00:03:18,084 --> 00:03:21,474
partners, with complete trust
and a common goal.

51
00:03:22,124 --> 00:03:25,844
Michael Schiavo, now her legal
guardian, took her to California for

52
00:03:25,864 --> 00:03:27,654
an experimental brain implant.

53
00:03:28,284 --> 00:03:29,194
It didn't help.

54
00:03:31,734 --> 00:03:37,694
In 1993, a malpractice case brought
money: around seven hundred thousand

55
00:03:37,714 --> 00:03:41,014
dollars, held in a court-supervised
trust for her care, and three

56
00:03:41,034 --> 00:03:43,334
hundred thousand for her husband.

57
00:03:43,354 --> 00:03:46,414
That February the two
families stopped speaking.

58
00:03:46,434 --> 00:03:48,784
Each came to accuse the other
of acting for money.

59
00:03:49,174 --> 00:03:52,494
The trial judge's view was that
neither side was exempt from finger

60
00:03:52,514 --> 00:03:53,024
pointing.

61
00:03:55,444 --> 00:03:59,844
In 1994, on medical advice, he
declined treatment for an infection

62
00:03:59,864 --> 00:04:01,304
and put in a do-not-resuscitate order.

63
00:04:01,324 --> 00:04:05,524
When her nursing home
objected, he backed down.

64
00:04:05,544 --> 00:04:09,024
Her parents went to court to have
him removed as her guardian.

65
00:04:09,044 --> 00:04:14,424
They failed. Their hope, the judge
later found, was that he would

66
00:04:14,444 --> 00:04:16,844
divorce her and let them
become her guardians.

67
00:04:16,864 --> 00:04:21,784
They had also encouraged him to get
on with his life, and in time

68
00:04:21,804 --> 00:04:25,654
he did: he had a new partner,
and children with her.

69
00:04:27,154 --> 00:04:32,234
In May 1998, eight years after her
collapse, Michael Schiavo asked

70
00:04:32,254 --> 00:04:34,664
a court to let her feeding
tube be removed.

71
00:04:34,974 --> 00:04:37,704
He said she wouldn't have wanted to
be kept alive like that.

72
00:04:37,974 --> 00:04:39,324
Her parents opposed him.

73
00:04:41,324 --> 00:04:43,594
So, the first weight on the scale.

74
00:04:44,184 --> 00:04:46,214
A young woman with nothing in writing.

75
00:04:46,704 --> 00:04:48,774
A husband who was her legal guardian.

76
00:04:49,344 --> 00:04:51,634
Parents who believed she
was still there.

77
00:04:52,044 --> 00:04:54,414
And two families who no longer spoke.

78
00:05:00,552 --> 00:05:05,322
In January 2000, Judge George Greer
heard the case, without a jury:

79
00:05:05,912 --> 00:05:08,062
eighteen witnesses, over a week.

80
00:05:08,672 --> 00:05:09,822
There was nothing in writing.

81
00:05:10,292 --> 00:05:14,232
The question the law put to him was
not what was best for her, but

82
00:05:14,252 --> 00:05:16,602
what Terri Schiavo herself
would have chosen.

83
00:05:17,712 --> 00:05:22,012
Florida's courts had put it this way: you
don't exercise another person's

84
00:05:22,032 --> 00:05:26,432
right to decide by choosing what the
state, the family or public opinion

85
00:05:26,452 --> 00:05:27,342
would prefer.

86
00:05:28,802 --> 00:05:33,522
Three witnesses described things she had
said: her husband, his brother

87
00:05:33,542 --> 00:05:34,102
and his sister-in-law.

88
00:05:34,122 --> 00:05:39,662
At a funeral lunch, her brother-in-law
testified, she said: if I ever

89
00:05:39,682 --> 00:05:41,882
go like that, just let me go.

90
00:05:41,902 --> 00:05:43,462
Don't leave me there.

91
00:05:43,482 --> 00:05:46,512
I don't want to be kept
alive on a machine.

92
00:05:48,972 --> 00:05:52,892
Her mother testified that Terri,
watching the news about Karen Ann

93
00:05:52,912 --> 00:05:57,432
Quinlan, a young woman kept alive on a
respirator, had said they should

94
00:05:57,452 --> 00:06:01,252
leave her alone. The judge found Terri
would have been eleven or twelve

95
00:06:01,272 --> 00:06:03,522
at the time, and gave it no weight.

96
00:06:05,182 --> 00:06:08,922
Judge Greer found her words to the
three of them reliable, and clear

97
00:06:08,942 --> 00:06:13,472
and convincing. In February 2000,
he granted the petition.

98
00:06:15,572 --> 00:06:17,962
One fact matters more than
any other here.

99
00:06:18,872 --> 00:06:20,902
Michael Schiavo did not
make the decision.

100
00:06:21,192 --> 00:06:24,772
Because the family disagreed, he asked
the court to make it in her

101
00:06:24,792 --> 00:06:29,272
place. From then on, the judge was in
effect her guardian, deciding

102
00:06:29,292 --> 00:06:30,542
what she would have chosen.

103
00:06:33,122 --> 00:06:35,112
The appeals court upheld the ruling.

104
00:06:35,782 --> 00:06:38,692
In April 2001, the tube was removed.

105
00:06:39,402 --> 00:06:42,542
Two days later her parents went to a
different judge, who ordered

106
00:06:42,562 --> 00:06:43,392
it put back.

107
00:06:44,712 --> 00:06:47,662
Her parents said she was more aware
than the doctors believed.

108
00:06:48,312 --> 00:06:53,482
In 2002, the court heard from five
doctors: two chosen by each side,

109
00:06:53,712 --> 00:06:54,722
and one by the court.

110
00:06:55,352 --> 00:06:58,522
Her parents' two said she was not
in a vegetative state.

111
00:06:58,952 --> 00:07:00,562
The other three said she was.

112
00:07:01,152 --> 00:07:02,702
The judge agreed with the three.

113
00:07:04,622 --> 00:07:08,502
Short clips from those exams, of her
seeming to respond and to follow

114
00:07:08,522 --> 00:07:11,252
a balloon, were later shown
around the world.

115
00:07:12,022 --> 00:07:13,112
We won't show them.

116
00:07:13,642 --> 00:07:15,132
She couldn't consent to that.

117
00:07:16,022 --> 00:07:19,682
The appeals judges watched all of the
tapes, and the guardian who

118
00:07:19,702 --> 00:07:24,022
later reviewed them found moments of
response, but not repeated or

119
00:07:24,042 --> 00:07:24,972
consistent ones.

120
00:07:27,172 --> 00:07:32,042
In June 2003, the appeals court upheld
the ruling again, and wrote:

121
00:07:32,472 --> 00:07:36,032
if Mrs. Schiavo were our own daughter,
we could not but hold to such

122
00:07:36,052 --> 00:07:40,112
a faith. But this case is not about the
aspirations that loving parents

123
00:07:40,132 --> 00:07:41,402
have for their children.

124
00:07:42,072 --> 00:07:45,482
It is about Theresa Schiavo's right
to make her own decision.

125
00:07:48,402 --> 00:07:49,992
So, the second weight.

126
00:07:50,502 --> 00:07:54,282
Her words reached the court years
later, from her husband and his

127
00:07:54,302 --> 00:07:59,432
family. But a judge heard every
witness, five doctors examined her,

128
00:07:59,762 --> 00:08:02,312
and every appeal reached
the same answer.

129
00:08:03,202 --> 00:08:07,042
And the courts found something else:
that both sides were acting out

130
00:08:07,062 --> 00:08:07,932
of love for her.

131
00:08:14,020 --> 00:08:17,790
In October 2003, the tube was
removed a second time.

132
00:08:18,280 --> 00:08:22,080
Six days later, Florida's legislature
passed a law written so that

133
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:26,140
it fit only her case, letting the
governor, Jeb Bush, order it put

134
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:28,250
back. He did, that day.

135
00:08:29,570 --> 00:08:33,220
The law also brought in a new
guardian for her, Jay Wolfson.

136
00:08:33,810 --> 00:08:36,660
He read nearly thirty thousand
pages of court records.

137
00:08:36,830 --> 00:08:39,970
He found the evidence of her
condition compelling, and wrote that

138
00:08:39,990 --> 00:08:41,760
the law had done its job well.

139
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:48,720
In September 2004, Florida's Supreme
Court struck down Terri's Law,

140
00:08:48,740 --> 00:08:51,750
unanimously, for breaching the
separation of powers.

141
00:08:52,280 --> 00:08:55,060
The chief justice wrote that the
court could fully understand the

142
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:58,910
family's grief. But our hearts,
she wrote, are not the law.

143
00:09:01,590 --> 00:09:05,430
On March the eighteenth, 2005, the
tube was removed a third time.

144
00:09:05,450 --> 00:09:09,830
Florida's House voted to intervene
again; its Senate voted no.

145
00:09:09,850 --> 00:09:14,130
Two days later, on Palm Sunday, the
Senate passed the federal act

146
00:09:14,150 --> 00:09:17,800
by voice vote. The House followed just
after midnight, two hundred

147
00:09:18,370 --> 00:09:20,550
and three to fifty-eight.

148
00:09:20,570 --> 00:09:23,610
The White House said that where there
are serious doubts, the law

149
00:09:23,630 --> 00:09:25,680
should have a presumption
in favor of life.

150
00:09:26,1000 --> 00:09:29,170
The act didn't order the
tube put back.

151
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:32,840
It gave a federal court the power to
look at the case again, from

152
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:33,470
the beginning.

153
00:09:34,850 --> 00:09:39,790
The federal courts refused: a district
judge, the appeals court, and

154
00:09:39,810 --> 00:09:42,980
then the Supreme Court, which
declined to step in.

155
00:09:43,450 --> 00:09:47,790
One appeals judge, Stanley Birch, wrote
that Congress and the President

156
00:09:47,810 --> 00:09:51,340
had acted in a manner demonstrably at
odds with the Constitution.

157
00:09:52,150 --> 00:09:56,130
Another, Charles Wilson, dissented: her
death would end the case without

158
00:09:56,150 --> 00:09:57,940
a fair chance to consider it.

159
00:10:00,380 --> 00:10:03,250
Most Americans thought the
politicians should have stayed out.

160
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:07,370
In a CBS poll that week,
eighty-two percent said so.

161
00:10:10,590 --> 00:10:14,830
Terri Schiavo died on March the
thirty-first, 2005, thirteen days

162
00:10:14,850 --> 00:10:15,770
after the tube was removed.

163
00:10:15,790 --> 00:10:17,400
She was forty-one.

164
00:10:20,920 --> 00:10:25,200
The autopsy found her brain weighed
six hundred and fifteen grams,

165
00:10:25,220 --> 00:10:27,460
less than half what it should have.

166
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:31,540
The parts that handle sight had
died: she was blind.

167
00:10:31,560 --> 00:10:34,160
She could not have swallowed safely.

168
00:10:34,180 --> 00:10:39,760
She died of dehydration, the medical
examiner found, not starvation.

169
00:10:39,780 --> 00:10:43,440
But the neuropathologist added that
no examination of a brain can

170
00:10:43,460 --> 00:10:45,970
prove or disprove a vegetative state.

171
00:10:48,610 --> 00:10:49,860
So, the third weight.

172
00:10:50,270 --> 00:10:54,410
A governor, a legislature, Congress
and the President all stepped

173
00:10:54,430 --> 00:10:59,380
in for one woman. Every court that
looked at what they did said no.

174
00:10:59,850 --> 00:11:02,460
And the autopsy found what the
judge had been told.

175
00:11:08,822 --> 00:11:12,802
Four months after Terri Schiavo
collapsed, the US Supreme Court decided

176
00:11:12,822 --> 00:11:17,152
the case of Nancy Cruzan, a young woman
in Missouri in a similar state.

177
00:11:17,602 --> 00:11:21,002
It said a state may demand clear and
convincing evidence of what the

178
00:11:21,022 --> 00:11:25,382
patient wanted. Missouri did, and found
that what she had said wasn't

179
00:11:25,402 --> 00:11:29,212
specific enough. Terri Schiavo's
words were no more specific.

180
00:11:29,902 --> 00:11:31,272
Florida's courts found them enough.

181
00:11:31,822 --> 00:11:35,582
In the same case, Justice Scalia wrote
that questions like these should

182
00:11:35,602 --> 00:11:39,692
be decided by the citizens of each state,
through their elected representatives.

183
00:11:42,432 --> 00:11:45,292
Florida's law sets an order for who
speaks for you if you haven't

184
00:11:45,312 --> 00:11:49,202
chosen anyone: a court-appointed
guardian, then your spouse, then

185
00:11:49,652 --> 00:11:51,532
your adult children,
then your parents.

186
00:11:51,552 --> 00:11:56,172
And the decision has to be the one
you would have made, proven by

187
00:11:56,192 --> 00:12:00,952
clear and convincing evidence, or, if
no one can know that, what's

188
00:12:00,972 --> 00:12:02,062
in your best interests.

189
00:12:03,542 --> 00:12:07,432
But the people closest to us are not
as good at this as we'd hope.

190
00:12:07,562 --> 00:12:11,422
A review of sixteen studies found that
surrogates predicted patients'

191
00:12:11,442 --> 00:12:15,162
choices correctly sixty-eight percent
of the time, and that talking

192
00:12:15,182 --> 00:12:18,072
it over beforehand didn't make
them any more accurate.

193
00:12:19,932 --> 00:12:21,602
England asks a different question.

194
00:12:22,692 --> 00:12:26,472
Since 1993, when the House of Lords
let doctors stop tube-feeding

195
00:12:26,492 --> 00:12:30,312
Tony Bland, left in a vegetative state
by the Hillsborough disaster,

196
00:12:30,332 --> 00:12:35,392
English law has treated tube feeding as
medical treatment, and asked

197
00:12:35,412 --> 00:12:37,402
what is in the patient's
best interests.

198
00:12:38,452 --> 00:12:41,042
The family is consulted,
but doesn't decide.

199
00:12:41,712 --> 00:12:43,922
The patient's wishes and
beliefs count.

200
00:12:44,652 --> 00:12:47,682
And if there's real
disagreement, a court decides.

201
00:12:50,142 --> 00:12:52,952
Not everyone accepts that tube
feeding is treatment.

202
00:12:53,662 --> 00:12:59,092
In 2004, Pope John Paul the Second called
it a natural means of preserving

203
00:12:59,282 --> 00:13:01,442
life, not a medical act.

204
00:13:01,462 --> 00:13:04,462
Twenty-three disability rights groups
said a feeding tube is low-tech

205
00:13:04,482 --> 00:13:08,122
support, and that people who use one
can live full and meaningful

206
00:13:08,142 --> 00:13:08,772
lives.

207
00:13:10,712 --> 00:13:12,962
And the diagnosis itself can be wrong.

208
00:13:13,252 --> 00:13:17,292
In one London unit, forty-three percent of
patients referred as vegetative

209
00:13:17,312 --> 00:13:19,302
turned out to have been misdiagnosed.

210
00:13:19,672 --> 00:13:23,632
In her case, though, three of five
doctors, the trial judge and the

211
00:13:23,652 --> 00:13:26,122
autopsy all pointed the same way.

212
00:13:28,362 --> 00:13:29,892
So, the fourth weight.

213
00:13:30,582 --> 00:13:33,412
American law tries to give you the
choice you would have made.

214
00:13:33,622 --> 00:13:37,282
But most people never put it in
writing, and the people who love them

215
00:13:37,302 --> 00:13:39,192
guess wrong a third of the time.

216
00:13:45,260 --> 00:13:49,670
First, the strongest case for her
husband and the courts.

217
00:13:50,500 --> 00:13:54,770
The law had already answered this
question: the decision is hers,

218
00:13:55,400 --> 00:13:58,920
and when she can't speak, the people
closest to her report what she

219
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:02,730
said, and if they disagree,
a judge weighs it.

220
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:04,250
That's what happened.

221
00:14:04,980 --> 00:14:09,010
A judge heard eighteen witnesses and found
her words clear and convincing.

222
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:12,590
The appeals courts reviewed
it again and again.

223
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:17,220
Five doctors examined her, and the
autopsy found a brain less than

224
00:14:17,240 --> 00:14:21,820
half its weight. To overrule all that,
you have to say her words don't

225
00:14:21,840 --> 00:14:26,060
count because they were spoken, not
written, or that her family's

226
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:27,470
grief should outweigh her.

227
00:14:28,990 --> 00:14:31,160
Now the strongest case
for her parents.

228
00:14:31,930 --> 00:14:35,350
The only evidence of what she wanted
was a few remarks made in her

229
00:14:35,370 --> 00:14:38,880
twenties, recalled years later by
her husband and his family.

230
00:14:39,290 --> 00:14:43,090
A guardian appointed by the court in
1998 thought her husband's word

231
00:14:43,110 --> 00:14:46,780
alone wasn't enough, and Missouri
would have demanded more.

232
00:14:47,590 --> 00:14:52,430
Surrogates guess wrong a third of the
time, and the diagnosis is sometimes

233
00:14:52,450 --> 00:14:56,630
wrong. Her parents were willing to
care for her for the rest of her

234
00:14:56,650 --> 00:15:01,610
life. When the evidence is that thin,
and the outcome is death, the

235
00:15:01,630 --> 00:15:07,280
law should lean toward life, and see food
and water as care, not treatment.

236
00:15:08,940 --> 00:15:09,890
Here's our verdict.

237
00:15:10,460 --> 00:15:15,760
On who decides: when a family can't
agree, a judge should, on the

238
00:15:15,780 --> 00:15:18,390
person's own words and the
medical evidence.

239
00:15:19,120 --> 00:15:21,960
Not a legislature, and not
whoever is loudest.

240
00:15:21,980 --> 00:15:25,100
Our confidence is eighty percent.

241
00:15:25,120 --> 00:15:29,640
What would change our mind: evidence
that judges in contested cases

242
00:15:29,660 --> 00:15:33,880
are less faithful to patients' wishes
than families or doctors would

243
00:15:33,900 --> 00:15:34,330
be.

244
00:15:37,990 --> 00:15:41,200
On Terri Schiavo: the courts
got her case right.

245
00:15:41,450 --> 00:15:45,210
Her words were thin, but they were
tested harder than in almost any

246
00:15:45,230 --> 00:15:48,300
case before or since, and
the medicine agreed.

247
00:15:49,090 --> 00:15:53,150
Our confidence is seventy percent,
lower, because remembered remarks

248
00:15:53,170 --> 00:15:56,120
are weak evidence, and a
life was at stake.

249
00:15:56,750 --> 00:16:00,510
What would change our mind: credible
evidence that the testimony about

250
00:16:00,530 --> 00:16:04,190
her words was invented, or that she
was minimally conscious after

251
00:16:04,210 --> 00:16:04,720
all.

252
00:16:08,380 --> 00:16:12,520
And on the politicians: Florida and
Congress were wrong to step into

253
00:16:12,540 --> 00:16:15,310
one family's case after the
courts had ruled.

254
00:16:15,940 --> 00:16:19,000
Our confidence is eighty-five percent.

255
00:16:19,020 --> 00:16:22,780
What would change our mind: evidence
that the courts' process really

256
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:27,070
was deficient, that something that
mattered was never heard.

257
00:16:27,800 --> 00:16:31,840
And one thing nearly everyone in this
case would agree on: write down

258
00:16:31,860 --> 00:16:35,110
what you would want, and tell
the person you trust.

259
00:16:39,030 --> 00:16:40,120
That's our verdict.

260
00:16:40,690 --> 00:16:43,960
It's open, which means it can change.

261
00:16:44,390 --> 00:16:48,320
If you think we got it wrong, make
your case in the comments.

262
00:16:48,990 --> 00:16:51,780
We'll answer the strongest one
in the next episode.

263
00:16:55,570 --> 00:16:59,710
Next case: in 2010, a
sixteen-year-old in New York was accused of

264
00:16:59,730 --> 00:17:01,330
robbing a man of his backpack.

265
00:17:01,350 --> 00:17:03,570
He said he didn't do it.

266
00:17:03,590 --> 00:17:07,410
His family couldn't pay his bail, and
because he was on probation,

267
00:17:07,430 --> 00:17:10,030
a judge soon held him without bail.

268
00:17:10,050 --> 00:17:15,350
He spent three years on Rikers Island,
much of it in solitary, and

269
00:17:15,370 --> 00:17:19,960
never had a trial. Should anyone be
jailed for years before a trial?
